/* data/learn-c/phy-11-04.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-11-04.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-04"] = {"4.1":{"s":"This chapter links force to motion. It gives Newton's three laws, the idea of a frame of reference, the main kinds of force, and then work and energy, momentum, collisions, torque, equilibrium and the centre of mass. These ideas explain how objects move, push each other and stay balanced."},"4.2":{"s":"Aristotle believed a force is needed to keep a body moving, because moving things stop when you stop pushing. This is wrong. Galileo showed that things stop because friction, air drag or other opposing forces act on them. A body would go on moving at constant velocity if no unbalanced force acted on it, which is the idea behind Newton's first law.","w":["Constant velocity needs no net force; a force is needed only to CHANGE the velocity."]},"4.3":{"s":"First law: a body stays at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless an unbalanced force acts on it. Second law: the rate of change of momentum is proportional to the applied force, and it follows the direction of the force. For constant mass this gives force equals mass times acceleration. Third law: to every action there is an equal and opposite reaction, acting on different bodies.","f":[{"x":"F = dp/dt","n":"Second law: force = rate of change of linear momentum p = m*v.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.3, p.48"},{"x":"F = m*a","n":"Second law for a body of constant mass m (not valid when the mass changes, as in a rocket).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.3, p.48"}],"w":["Action and reaction act on DIFFERENT bodies, so they never cancel each other.","F = ma is a special case: when mass changes, use F = dp/dt."]},"4.4":{"s":"Motion is relative, so we describe it from a frame of reference. A frame that is at rest or moves at constant velocity is an inertial frame, and Newton's laws hold in it. A frame that accelerates, such as a turning bus, is non-inertial. In such a frame bodies seem to feel extra forces that do not come from any real interaction."},"4.5":{"s":"Forces can be gravitational, electromagnetic, strong nuclear or weak nuclear. Contact forces (normal reaction, friction, tension) are electromagnetic in origin. In a non-inertial frame we add a pseudo force, equal to minus mass times the frame's acceleration, so that Newton's second law still works. A lift accelerating upward makes your apparent weight larger. Work done against a conservative force is stored as potential energy.","f":[{"x":"g = G*M/R^2","n":"Acceleration due to gravity at the Earth's surface; M and R = mass and radius of the Earth.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.5, p.51"},{"x":"F_pseudo = -m*a","n":"Pseudo force on a body of mass m in a frame accelerating with acceleration a.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.5, p.53"},{"x":"W_apparent = m*(g + a)","n":"Apparent weight in a lift accelerating UPWARD with a; use m*(g - a) when it accelerates downward.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.5, p.53"},{"x":"W = F*s*cos(theta)","n":"Work done by a constant force F over displacement s; theta = angle between F and s.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.5, p.54"}],"w":["Pseudo forces appear only in accelerating frames; they have no reaction partner.","In a lift, apparent weight is m(g + a) going up faster or slowing while going down, and m(g − a) in the opposite cases."]},"4.6":{"s":"The net work done on a body by all forces equals the change in its kinetic energy. A force along the motion increases the speed and does positive work; an opposing force decreases it. With friction or air drag, which are non-conservative, some work is lost as heat, so the mechanical energy is not conserved.","f":[{"x":"(1/2)*m*v^2 - (1/2)*m*u^2 = F*s","n":"Work-energy theorem for a constant force F along a straight line: u = initial and v = final speed.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.6, p.55"}],"w":["Include ALL forces in the work, including friction; a force at right angles to the motion does no work."]},"4.7":{"s":"If no net external force acts on a system, its total linear momentum stays constant. The forces between the parts (internal forces) do not change the total. This holds for collisions and explosions. To solve problems with several bodies, draw a free body diagram for each: show only the forces acting on that body, then write Newton's second law for it.","f":[{"x":"a = (m_2 - m_1)*g/(m_1 + m_2)","n":"Atwood machine (light string over a pulley): acceleration of masses m_1 and m_2, with m_2 > m_1.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.7, p.57"},{"x":"T = 2*m_1*m_2*g/(m_1 + m_2)","n":"Tension in the string of the Atwood machine.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.7, p.58"}],"w":["Momentum is conserved only for the whole system: add the momenta of all bodies, with signs.","A free body diagram shows forces ON the body, never the forces it exerts on others."]},"4.8":{"s":"In a collision, total momentum is always conserved. In an elastic collision kinetic energy is also conserved. In an inelastic collision some kinetic energy is lost. In a perfectly inelastic collision the bodies stick together and the loss is greatest. The coefficient of restitution e, the ratio of the speed of separation to the speed of approach, is 1 for elastic and 0 for perfectly inelastic, and between 0 and 1 otherwise.","f":[{"x":"e = (v_2 - v_1)/(u_1 - u_2)","n":"Coefficient of restitution for a head-on collision: u = speeds before, v = speeds after.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.8, p.59"},{"x":"v_1 = ((m_1 - m_2)/(m_1 + m_2))*u_1 + (2*m_2/(m_1 + m_2))*u_2","n":"Final velocity of m_1 after a head-on ELASTIC collision.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.8, p.60"},{"x":"v_2 = ((m_2 - m_1)/(m_1 + m_2))*u_2 + (2*m_1/(m_1 + m_2))*u_1","n":"Final velocity of m_2 after the same elastic collision.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.8, p.60"},{"x":"v = (m_1*u_1 + m_2*u_2)/(m_1 + m_2)","n":"Common velocity after a perfectly inelastic collision.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.8, p.61"},{"x":"Delta KE = (1/2)*(m_1*m_2/(m_1 + m_2))*(u_1 - u_2)^2","n":"Kinetic energy lost in a perfectly inelastic head-on collision.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.8, p.61"}],"w":["Put signs on the velocities: take one direction as positive before using the formulas.","Equal masses in a head-on elastic collision simply swap velocities."]},"4.9":{"s":"Impulse is the effect of a large force acting for a very short time, such as a bat hitting a ball. It equals the force multiplied by the time, and it is equal to the change in momentum. For a force that changes with time, the impulse is the area under the force-time graph. Impulse is a vector in the direction of the force.","f":[{"x":"J = F*t = p_2 - p_1","n":"Impulse of a constant force F acting for time t: equal to the change of momentum.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.9, p.63"},{"x":"J = integral of F dt","n":"Impulse of a force that changes with time: the area under the F-t graph.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.9, p.63"}],"w":["A longer contact time means a smaller average force for the same change in momentum: this is why a cricketer draws the hands back."]},"4.10":{"s":"Torque is the turning effect of a force about an axis. Its size is the force times the perpendicular distance from the axis to the line of the force, so it depends on where and in which direction the force acts. It is largest when the force is at 90° to the line from the axis. It is zero when the force passes through the axis, and it is a vector found with the right hand rule.","f":[{"x":"tau = r*F*sin(theta)","n":"Torque of a force F at position r from the axis; theta = smaller angle between r and F. Vector form: tau = r × F.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.10, p.66"}],"w":["A force through the axis (θ = 0° or 180°) gives zero torque, however large.","Torque depends on the chosen axis; always state which one."]},"4.11":{"s":"A couple is a pair of equal and opposite forces whose lines of action are different. It produces pure rotation and no translation, because the forces add to zero. The moment of a couple is the force times the perpendicular distance between the two lines, and it is the same about every axis. A couple can be balanced only by another couple.","f":[{"x":"tau = F*r","n":"Moment of a couple: F = size of each force, r = perpendicular distance between their lines of action.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.11, p.66"}],"w":["The torque of a couple does not depend on the axis chosen or on where the forces are applied."]},"4.12":{"s":"A body is in mechanical equilibrium when the net force on it is zero, so it has no acceleration. For a rigid body the net torque must also be zero. Equilibrium can be stable, unstable or neutral. In stable equilibrium the potential energy is a minimum, and a small push makes the body return. In unstable equilibrium it is a maximum, and in neutral equilibrium it stays constant.","f":[{"x":"Sum of F = 0","n":"Condition for translational equilibrium; for a rigid body also the sum of torques = 0.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.12, p.67"},{"x":"dU/dx = 0, d^2U/dx^2 > 0 (stable), d^2U/dx^2 < 0 (unstable)","n":"Equilibrium positions from the potential energy U(x); second derivative zero means neutral.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.12, p.68"}],"w":["For a ladder or a beam, use both conditions: forces balance AND torques about any point balance."]},"4.13":{"s":"The centre of mass is the point where the whole mass of a system can be treated as concentrated when applying Newton's laws. For particles it is the mass-weighted average of positions; for a continuous body, use integration. It lies closer to the heavier mass. For a symmetric body of uniform density it is at the geometric centre. It need not lie inside the body, and only external forces change its motion.","f":[{"x":"x_cm = (m_1*x_1 + m_2*x_2 + ... + m_n*x_n)/M","n":"Centre of mass of n particles along x; M = total mass. Same form for y and z.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.69"},{"x":"x_cm = (m_1*x_1 + m_2*x_2)/(m_1 + m_2)","n":"Two particles.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.71"},{"x":"v_cm = (m_1*v_1 + m_2*v_2 + ...)/M","n":"Velocity of the centre of mass: total momentum divided by total mass.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.72"},{"x":"y = 4*R/(3*pi)","n":"Centre of mass of a thin semicircular disc of radius R, measured from the diameter. A thin semicircular ring has 2R/pi.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.70"},{"x":"y = 3*R/8","n":"Solid hemisphere of radius R, from the flat face. A hemispherical shell has R/2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.70"},{"x":"y = h/4 (solid cone), y = h/3 (hollow cone)","n":"Right circular cone of height h, measured from the base.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.70"}],"w":["Choose the origin and the positive directions first, and give each coordinate its sign.","For a body with a hole, treat the hole as a negative mass."]},"4.14":{"s":"The centre of gravity is the point where the total weight of a body acts, the point about which the torque of gravity is zero. For a uniform gravitational field it coincides with the centre of mass. The two differ only when gravity varies over the body, which is negligible for ordinary objects. A suspended body hangs with its centre of gravity vertically below the point of support.","w":["Centre of gravity and centre of mass are the same point in a uniform gravitational field."]}};
